#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129027 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129208 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0026a41c-1953-55c7-8abe-d387671576c4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Mass or Charge Over a Type I or Type II Region"}] :question/id #uuid "27249c57-7f17-5e3e-9a21-0518320f5076" :question/math-academy-id "e-8192" :question/problem "![](images/73/73e2bd9a47c9d16f93d5240db3c4babc36f9fbe703ac9e0af449acaa7528f804.png)\n\nA triangular plate occupies the region ![](images/6e/6e8362574defb66da2f3a81265f216d5e82fe6f01d9db6dc6fde3879b58d137a.png) enclosed between the lines ![](images/0b/0b759759dc6e40e6a983b68c1b1ac821c304183d638e47347d3dc15bb6d43711.png) and ![](images/e5/e5edcbd505cc63ee5461688fda2a101b0e6d529c2cb8185906f9ee564108415d.png) as shown above. The plate's mass density function ![](images/8c/8cd15d36128c5a26b2b25f57faa5cc41ecbe3e94af66e9c053a3da0a5425bac4.png) is given by ![](images/d8/d83addab9edd09882eb2af07d7ceb9facb3c4ca85179e3ad771581f00e3c932f.png) Find the total mass of the triangular plate." :question/worked-solution "The mass of the triangular plate occupying the region ![](images/3c/3c51a5e31ce9f37895e4e38935b015ba8fd40373baa1c8735bd63f3592968af9.png) with mass density ![](images/ca/ca272ba1efbdf1cd3d7c6c0d41a91233ce4a64659e5c96d040f9ff2da45b6ddf.png) is given by\n\n![](images/45/457c03e2f0a2295eaf404978491ea77c038507ba33bb1125a2a05952d0565af3.png)\n\nNotice that ![](images/05/05ff793b0aee1f05a79216f9e7f119afa544749c81077937f8f06a353f6ef4eb.png) is a type I region. So, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n![](images/4c/4c3142a8a4beae1e14a9ca2a611a7101d9a2a4cfe553109ae20a1d1b0dd78f64.png)\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to ![](images/d2/d2a923f8537e18206a972cc41916ae6f9ee26abc93734cd0a73c50ffc165e08a.png) treating ![](images/0e/0ea8d498dfe5ba44cab2ca474bceae4ef3205271d683d0fdeedf7ffd68a22a43.png) as a constant:\n\n![](images/a9/a9b1d2210f33a1242fccf6517fbc63783381f19cdc48daf78685900497b0829f.png)\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to ![](images/73/73c3b5d4a287ad14dceb771dbd299c9effc9f3aff16c0f4b4051737ed99b36ae.png)\n\n![](images/3e/3e75cffa3638e6260cbe5493a8277a484fe594d41e20ad55588da0b2dcf21fca.png)\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the plate is ![](images/32/327bb4cc2df638a85e6bbe52eeda604b942f097b691c0c2125d579596dfe54a8.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129028 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129209 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65ae2b65-4d0c-5a25-89f8-e186eda19d33" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Mass or Charge Over a Rectangular Domain"}] :question/id #uuid "d565cc1a-f78f-5712-8a03-df4d4e8f3e21" :question/math-academy-id "e-8193" :question/problem "Find the total charge $Q$ of a metal plate that occupies the region $D = \\{ (x,y) : 0 \\le x \\le 1, \\, 0 \\le y \\le 2\\}$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y) = 4 - 3y^2 + 2x.$" :question/worked-solution "The total charge $Q$ of a plate that occupies the region $D = \\{ (x,y) : 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\, 0 \\leq y \\leq 2\\}$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y) = 4 - 3y^2 + 2x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & ={∬}_{D}(4-3{y}^{2}+2x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express our double integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} (4 - 3y^2 + 2x) \\:\\textrm{d}A\n= \\int_{0}^2 \\left[ \\int_{0}^1 (4 - 3y^2 + 2x) \\:\\textrm{d}x \\right] \\textrm{d}y\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4-3{y}^{2}+2x)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[4x-3{y}^{2}x+{x}^{2}]_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[(4(1)-3{y}^{2}(1)+(1)^{2})-(4(0)-3{y}^{2}(0)-(0)^{2})]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(5-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(5-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y & =[5y-{y}^{3}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(5(2)-(2)^{3})-(5(0)-(0)^{3}) \\\\  & =(10-8)-0 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the charge of the plate is $Q=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129029 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129210 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60ac666d-7325-57a6-b4cb-b702765344c8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Mass or Charge Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "92b9b5ea-1153-580a-a03a-94338f30fe3e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8195" :question/problem "![](images/f0/f0db928874dba8d872cde46d1467686010f3484765b2d7e39807b5a32a131b09.png)\n\nFind the total charge $Q$ of a copper plate that occupies the region $D$ shown above, and has charge density \n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y) = \\dfrac{4x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}  .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the region $D$ represents one-quarter of the disk of radius $3$ centered at \n$\\left(0,0\\right).$\n\nThe total charge $Q$ of an object occupying the region $D$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y) =  \\dfrac{4x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}$ is given by\n\n$$\nQ = \\iint\\limits_{D} \\dfrac{4x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}  \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\rho(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ \\rho(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nThe following equations connect Cartesian and polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the curve $x^2+y^2=9$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2} & =9 \\\\ r & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 3, \\: \\pi \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{4x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [{\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\,\\text{d}r]\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (\\frac{9}{2}-0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =18[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =18[-1-0] \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total charge of the quarter-disk is $Q = - 18.$"}]}
